  "text_md": "Who lukes to the lefte syde, wheune his horse launches. With the lyghte of the sonne men myghtesee his ly vere. Morte Arthure, MS. Lincoln, f. 80.\n\nLAUNDE. A plain place in a wood ; an unploughed plain ; a park ; a lawn. \" Saltus, a lawnd,\" Nominale MS. Now is Gij to a launde y-go,\n\nYf that y sufur the wylle\n\nTo holde me 8 lavour and bason to my honde.\n\n144. (3) A dish composed of a kind of sea-weed well washed and boiled. It is also called laver^ bread, Kennett, MS. Lansd. 1033. Laver a hanging lip.\n\nWher the dragoun duelled tho. Gy of\n\nWarwike, p. 262.\n\nFor to hunt at the hartes in thas hye laundes In Glamorgane with glee, thare gladchipe was evere. Morte Arthure, MS. Lincoln , f. 53. LAUNDER. Any kind of gutter or channel\n\nLaver lip, 9 banging lip. VERD.\n\nThat tay after thaym ne went To du thayr laverd comandement.\n\nLAUNDER. (1) Any kind of or channel for conveying water. Var: dial. gutter\n\nWavwick , Middlehill MS: Guv\n\n(2) A washer. to wash. Buandiére, launderer_ Hollybands   Dictionarie, 1593 Laundring gold , washing it. Also,\n\nLAVEROCK. The Lyric Poetry, pp. 26, 40 ; Reliq. Antiq. i. 86 ; Wright's Purgatory, p. 55 ; laverkes, Beves of Hamtoun, p. 138, Sche made many a wondir soune,\n\nLAUNDRE A laundress. Palsgrave.\n\nLAUP. To Yorksh. leap.\n\nwondir soune,\n\nSumtyme liche unto the cok, Sumlyme unto the laverok. Gower, MS. Sac.\n\nlaurel. {Lat.) The laureatship at our universities was a degree in grammar, including poetry and rhetoric, so called because the person who graduated was presented with a wreath of laurel. LAUREOLE. Spurge-laurel. {A.-N.)\n\nGower, MS . Soc.\n\nTyrlery lorpyn,the laverocke songe, So meryly pypes the sparow\n\nThe cow brake lose, the rope ran home, Syr, God gyve yow morow. good\n\nLAUREOLE _ Spurge-laurel (A-n:)\n\nLAURER. A laurel. Chaucer\n\nBliss' 8 Bibl. Miscell. p. 54. LAVISH. Rank, as grass, &c. West.\n\nLAUS. Loose. (A-s:)\n\nLA-VOLTA. kind of verj active bouncing waltz,  formerly much in fashion. The man turned the woman round several times, and then assisted her in\n\nLAUTER The laurel. (A.-N)\n\nThat worthy was the lautcr to have Of poetrie, and the palme toatteyne.\n\nLydgate, MS. Ashnole 39,f. 48.\n\nCaught ; received. (A.-s) Thenne was Marie Joseph bitauzt, And he hỈr In spousaile lauzt\n\nLeave protestations now , and let us hie\n\nSoliman and Perseda, p. 214. North.\n\nMS. Coll. Trin. Cantab. [.67.\n\nLAVY. Lavish ; liberal. North.\n\nLAVALTOE. Same as Lavolta, q. v. For lo ! the liveless Jacks lavaltoes take At that sweet musick which themselves do\n\nLAW. (1) To givc hare start It is in very frequent use by at play. good Jaw, good boys\n\nAt that sweet musick which themsclves do make.\n\nBrome ed, 1661, p. 133. Songs,\n\n(2) hill, or eminence. North.\n\nLAVANDRE. laundress tretise for Reliq: Antiq. i.26.\n\nCustom manner. See Ellis, ii. 335.\n\nLow. North.\n\nLAVANT. A landspring: South.\n\nLAVAS:. Lavisb. Romeus and Juliet, P.20.\n\nhym was gode. But then he putte doune his hode On knees he fel downe latvc. MS. Cantab. Ff.\n\nLAVAST. Uninclosed stubble. Kent.\n\nLAVE. (1) The rest the remainder. North.\n\n(2) To lade or draw water. Chaucer. Also, to DOur, as in Perceval,  2250; to wash, Piers Ploughman, p. 273.\n\nLAWAND . Bowing humbling.\n\nsacramenles of haly kyrke, thof it be swa that thay hafebenecumbyrde in syne and with syne alle thaire lyfe tyme. MS. Lincoln A. 1. 17, f229.\n\nTo gutter, as a candle. Wilts.\n\nTo or down. Hall hang; flap\n\n(4) flap-eared. See Topsell's Beasts, p. 366 ; Hawkins, iii. 357 ; Lavelugged, Northumb. HoUoway has lapeared in use in Sussex and Hants. LAVEER. To work a ship against the wind.\n\nLAWE. (1) To laugh: Nomninale MS.\n\n(2) Rough violent ; brutal. West\n\nLWED Ignorant. Sce Laird.\n\nLAWESTE.\n\nLAVEER To work ship thc   wind. An old sea term against\n\nLA WESTE. The lowest. North.\n\nLenges all at laysere, and lokes one the wallys\n\nWhare they ware laweste the ledcs to assaille. LAWFUL-CASE. An interj. of surprize.\n\nLAVELL: The that covers the of the windpipe Still used in Devon. top\n\nLAVENDER To lay in lavender, to pawn. This is a very common phrase in old plays. To lay to pawne, aswe say to in lavander; Florio, p.27 lay\n\nLAWING.\n\nLow .\n\nHampole.\n\nLAWING. (1) Going t0 law. Linc.\n\n(2) Lawing of cutting the balls, or thrce claws of the fore-feet. dogs; out\n\nLAVENDREY. Washing.  (A.N)\n\nLAWLESS-MAN.\n\nLAVER. (1) Thc remainder North.\n\nLAWN. The same as Launde, 9\n\nto wash in. Palsgrave.   Also, Sce Florio, P. 89 ; Cotgrave, in Esguere_ Cathol. p. 154 Reliq   Antiq i 7; Davies' Ancient Rites, 1672, p. 130. And fulle glad, certys, thou schalt bee, Leg:\n\n(2) cistern, trough, or conduit, Laver to washe at, lavoyr\n\nLAWNDER. The sliding iron in thc fore-part of a plough. Var. dial\n\nbasin.\n\n",
